WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.085 --> 00:00:03.960
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.130
My name is Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.005
I'm a product manager
in security and privacy

00:00:07.005 --> 00:00:09.340
at Google, focused
on encryption.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.760
Today, we'll be talking
about encryption in transit,

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.820
how Google encrypts
data by default

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.022
to and within our
infrastructure.

00:00:16.022 --> 00:00:17.730
When you send a request
to a Google Cloud

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.625
Service like Google
Cloud Storage or Gmail,

00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:23.550
that request is first routed
via globally distributed system

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.470
called the Google front end.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.620
The Google front end encrypts
traffic from user to Google,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.830
and provides load balancing
and DDoS attack prevention.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.570
The Google front end
protects your data

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.700
using authentication,
which verifies

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.280
the source of your data,
integrity, which makes sure

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.340
the data that you send arrives
at its destination unaltered,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.160
and encryption, which makes
sure that your data is private.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.290
Any data that you send
to Google cloud service

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.210
is encrypted in
transit by default

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.860
from the user to
the Google front end

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.200
using transport layer security.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.700
This protection is
implemented using

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.400
boring SSL, which is an
open source implementation

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.140
of the TLS protocol.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.730
The user's browser
will automatically

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.580
negotiate the highest version of
the protocol for communication

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.030
with Google to ensure the
best possible security.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.252
So once your data's inside
Google, what happens?

00:01:09.252 --> 00:01:10.710
First, it's important
to understand

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.460
that not all data in
transit inside Google

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.520
is protected the same way.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.737
We apply different protections
to data in transit,

00:01:16.737 --> 00:01:18.570
depending on whether
or not it's transmitted

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.360
outside a physical boundary
controlled by Google.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.310
When data exits a physical
boundary controlled by Google,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.580
we assume that the network
can only be semi-trusted

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.314
and that data needs
further protection.

00:01:28.314 --> 00:01:29.730
On the other hand,
when data stays

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.454
within our physical
boundaries, we

00:01:31.454 --> 00:01:33.120
have rigorous security
measures in place

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.480
within our infrastructure,
which serve to secure that link.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.870
Let's talk about encryption at
the network layer, or Layer 3.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.180
Google cloud's virtual
network infrastructure

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.810
encrypts VM to VM traffic if it
crosses a physical boundary not

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.244
controlled by Google.

00:01:47.244 --> 00:01:49.410
The communicating hosts
perform a handshake and then

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.470
encrypt data using AS128.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.690
All data in transit at
Layer 3, whether or not

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.150
it leaves a physical
boundary, is authenticated.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.400
This protects a compromised
host from spoofing packets

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.080
on the network.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.380
Next, we encrypt at the
application layer, or Layer 7.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.410
Google's infrastructure provides
authentication, integrity,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.389
and encryption of
remote procedure calls

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.160
from service to
service when those

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.190
calls leave a physical
boundary controlled by Google.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.810
This is also a
handshake protocol,

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.970
and data's encrypted
using AS128.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.980
Within a physical boundary
controlled by Google,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.563
these remote procedure
calls are still

00:02:21.563 --> 00:02:24.330
protected with both
authentication and integrity.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.370
So to sum up our
story, Google uses

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.280
multiple layers of security to
protect your data in transit,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.169
including authentication,
integrity, and encryption.

00:02:32.169 --> 00:02:33.960
We encrypt and authenticate
data in transit

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.000
at one or more
network layers when

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.250
that data moves outside
of physical boundaries not

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.972
controlled by Google.

00:02:39.972 --> 00:02:42.180
Data in transit inside a
physical boundary controlled

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.240
by Google is authenticated,
but not necessarily encrypted,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.820
because rigorous physical
security measures are in place

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.080
by default. To learn more, read
our white paper, Encryption

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.550
in transit in Google Cloud.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

